```c
int A[128][128];   /* 4-byte int, stored row-major, page-aligned */
```
Page size is 512 bytes. A process is given **2 frames** for data (code is in a separate, always-resident frame), both initially empty, with LRU replacement. It zeroes the array with one of the two loops below.

```c
/* Loop X */ for (i = 0; i < 128; i++) for (j = 0; j < 128; j++) A[i][j] = 0;
/* Loop Y */ for (j = 0; j < 128; j++) for (i = 0; i < 128; i++) A[i][j] = 0;
```

How many page faults do Loop X and Loop Y generate, respectively?

```c
int A[64][64];   /* 4-byte int, row-major */
for (j = 0; j < 64; j++)
    for (i = 0; i < 64; i++)
        A[i][j] = i + j;
```
Page size is **1 KB** (1024 bytes). The array does **not** start on a page boundary: A[0][0] is at byte offset **512** within its page. The array gets **16 frames**, initially empty, with **LRU** replacement; code and the variables i, j are held elsewhere and never fault. How many page faults does the loop cause?

```c
int A[100][100];   /* 4-byte int, row-major, A[0][0] at the start of a page */
for (j = 0; j < 100; j++)
    for (i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        A[i][j] = 0;
```
Page size is **1 KB** (1024 bytes). The array gets **39 frames**, initially empty, with **LRU** replacement; code and loop variables never fault. How many page faults does the loop cause?
